{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS introduces enhanced network security policies - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced enhanced network security policies to improve the security posture for Kubernetes workloads and integrations with external destinations. These enhancements include the ability to centrally enforce network access filters across the entire cluster and leverage Domain Name System (DNS) based policies for securing egress traffic. EKS now supports Kubernetes NetworkPolicies in the Amazon VPC Container Network Interface (VPC CNI) plugin, enabling pod-to-pod communication segmentation at a namespace level. Cluster admins can now prevent unauthorized access to cluster-external resources using egress rules that filter traffic based on Fully Qualified Domain Names (FQDN). These new features are available in all commercial AWS Regions for new EKS clusters running Kubernetes version 1.29 or later, with support for existing clusters to follow. ClusterNetworkPolicy is available in all EKS cluster launch modes using VPC CNI v1.21.1 or later, while DNS-based policies are only supported in EKS Auto Mode-launched EC2 instances","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/amazon-eks-enhanced-network-security-policies/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices","skill_description":"Launches an EC2 instance with secure, cost-efficient defaults including AMI selection, burstable instance sizing, least-privilege IAM roles, hardened security groups, encrypted EBS volumes, and comprehensive tagging. Use when deploying new EC2 instances following AWS best practices for security and cost optimization.\n\nServices: ec2, iam, vpc, cloudwatch, ebs\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute\n*","skill_name":"launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"troubleshooting-efs","skill_description":"Diagnoses and resolves Amazon EFS issues including mount failures, NFS timeouts, permission errors, throughput problems, and burst credit exhaustion. Use when the user has an EFS file system that is not mounting, returning errors, performing slowly, or showing access denied.\n\n\nServices: efs, ec2, iam, kms, vpc, cloudwatch\nTasks: debug, troubleshoot, diagnose\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: storage\n*","skill_name":"troubleshooting-efs"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search CIS EKS benchmark kubelet control titles", "parent_id": "35c95314-737f-4b7f-a099-c437c26df131"}

